"<p>A generative AI demo can feel magical: one prompt, one response, and a new capability appears. A product has a different job. It must work across varied inputs, explain its limits, protect user data, fit into existing workflows, and behave well when the model or a dependency is unavailable.</p>\n<p>The path from demo to product is not simply “add a better prompt.” It is a process of defining the user task and engineering a system around the model.</p>\n<h2>Define what good means</h2>\n<p>Start by collecting representative examples of the task, including common requests, edge cases, and inputs that should be refused or escalated. Ask domain experts what a useful answer must contain and what errors would be costly.</p>\n<p>Turn those expectations into an evaluation set. Use a mix of deterministic checks—such as required fields or valid citations—and human review for qualities that are harder to reduce to a single score. Keep this set versioned so a change in model, prompt, retrieval, or application code can be compared against previous behavior.</p>\n<h2>Design for imperfect outputs</h2>\n<p>Models can omit details, misunderstand an instruction, or return a response that does not match the expected format. Validate structured outputs at the application boundary. Give users a way to correct or reject results, and keep important decisions reviewable by a person.</p>\n<p>Use the least amount of model freedom that solves the task. A constrained output schema, trusted context, and a small set of explicit actions are usually easier to test than a prompt that asks the model to handle everything.</p>\n<h2>Make speed and cost visible</h2>\n<p>Measure the full request path, not just generation time. Data retrieval, external APIs, retries, and serialization all contribute to latency. Track token usage and provider costs alongside quality so optimization does not silently make answers less useful.</p>\n<p>Caching can help for stable shared information, but user-specific or permission-sensitive responses need careful cache keys. Add timeouts and a clear fallback for provider errors so a temporary outage does not leave the user waiting without explanation.</p>\n<h2>Build the operational loop</h2>\n<p>Log enough to understand a run—versions, timings, validation results, and safe references to the inputs—without storing unnecessary sensitive content. Make it possible to trace a user-reported issue to the relevant code and configuration versions.</p>\n<p>After release, review feedback and real failure patterns. Update the evaluation set when new cases appear, then test improvements before rollout. This creates a learning loop instead of relying on occasional prompt edits made in response to anecdotes.</p>\n<p>Generative AI becomes a dependable product capability when its boundaries, evaluation, user experience, and operations are treated as first-class engineering work. The model is part of the feature; it is not the entire feature.</p>"